Subject: register VM fuses a strict compare against a just-loaded undefined

What this does:
  `x === undefined` and `x !== undefined` are box2d's default-argument guards
  and its most common strict compares. 0147 already turns the `undefined`
  operand into `ROP_load_undefined` when it is a global read, and 0145 already
  decides the compare from one tag test, but the two instructions still
  dispatch separately: the load writes `undefined` into a register that the
  compare immediately reads (8.86 M such pairs on the fixed kernel).

  When `regvm_fuse_branches` fuses a `ROP_binary` strict compare with the
  following branch, it now also looks one instruction further back. If that
  instruction is `ROP_load_undefined`, the loaded register has no other reader
  (its use count is two: the definition and this compare), and the loaded
  register is one of the compare's two operands, then the load and the compare
  are both redundant: with one operand known to be `undefined`, the strict
  compare is exactly a tag test on the other operand. The branch is rewritten
  to carry `REG_BIN_IS_UNDEF` or `REG_BIN_IS_NOT_UNDEF`, both operands become
  the other operand's value number, and the load and the compare are dropped.
  Dropping two adjacent instructions leaves that operand's register untouched
  from its definition through the branch, which is what makes reading it there
  sound without a liveness change; the operands must be distinct, because if
  both were the loaded register there is no other operand to test.

  The two pseudo sub-operations sit below `REG_CMP_INVERT` and above every real
  `REG_BIN_*` member, so no real sub-operation changes value and the sub byte
  stays a single byte. `regvm_cmp_opcode` returns -1 for them, so the encoder
  keeps the narrow `ROP_branch_cmp_reg_reg_s` form and the new test rides the
  existing compare-and-branch handlers: one tag test in the threaded
  `T_DEF_CMP_BRANCH` and in both monolith `REGVM_CMP_BRANCH` macros. The
  per-sense instantiations carry a constant sub-op and fold the test away, so
  only `branch_cmp_reg_reg(_s)` pays for the extra compare.

  Because the sub byte's meaning changes, `REGVM_FORMAT_VERSION` goes 13 -> 14,
  and the register bytecode blobs staged in 9999 are regenerated for the new
  format.

  Measured (`fixed.js`, 32 iterations, i7-8700), interleaved against 0149:
  23,066,148,676 -> 22,894,815,962 instructions (**-0.169 G, -0.73 %**), cycles
  median -0.7 %, against the plan's 0.07-0.09 G expected and 0.07 G floor. Gate
  run 22,896,286,096 / 10,952,645,402. Checksum `546.1008391043414` exact;
  diff-suite 398/0/0 mono and threaded; leak assertion clean; peak RSS 13248 KB;
  full test262 errors 50 (<= 64, unchanged).

